{#
  Date helpers for structured data. Imported by anything that emits a date into
  JSON-LD; nothing here renders on its own.

  THE RULE, and why it exists.

  schema.org dates are ISO 8601. EDTF is not ISO 8601 as soon as it does the one
  thing we keep it for: carry a qualifier. "1847?-03-27" says the year is
  uncertain and the day is not, which is exactly what the sources support for
  Alex Mentry, and it is not a value any consumer of our JSON-LD can read.

  So a template had two ways to be wrong and was taking the first: publish the
  EDTF string raw and emit an invalid date, or strip the qualifier and publish a
  certainty no source supports. Publishing an invalid date is wrong. Publishing
  a false certain one is worse, because it is legible. We publish neither: a
  qualified date is withheld from structured data and rendered on the page
  instead, where a reader can see the hedge.

  This is the rule the derived image links already follow. JSON-LD is an
  assertion to the rest of the web, so it carries only what the archive can
  stand behind.

  WHITELIST, NOT BLACKLIST. `certain()` tests for the three shapes schema.org
  accepts and returns nothing for everything else. Testing for '?~%' instead
  would pass every form nobody has thought of yet: intervals, sets, unspecified
  digits, seasons, and whatever EDTF grows next. Today 37 of the 976 stored EDTF
  values are intervals like '2005-11/2005-12', and every one of them is being
  published as a schema.org Date right now.
#}

{#- An ISO 8601 date parsed out of a printed one, or nothing. Returns '' for
    anything it cannot be sure of. -#}
{% macro iso(raw) %}
{%- set s = (raw ?? '')|trim|trim('.') -%}
{%- if s matches '/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/' -%}{{ s }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^\\d{4}$/' -%}{{ s }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}$/' -%}{{ s }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^(January|February|March|April|May|June|July|August|September|October|November|December)\\s+\\d{1,2},\\s*\\d{4}$/' -%}{{ s|date('Y-m-d') }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^(January|February|March|April|May|June|July|August|September|October|November|December)\\s+\\d{4}$/' -%}{{ s|date('Y-m') }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^(Mon|Tues|Wednes|Thurs|Fri|Satur|Sun)day,\\s+(January|February|March|April|May|June|July|August|September|October|November|December)\\s+\\d{1,2},\\s*\\d{4}$/' -%}{{ s|date('Y-m-d') }}
{%- elseif s matches '/^\\d{1,2}\\/\\d{1,2}\\/\\d{4}$/' -%}{{ s|date('Y-m-d') }}
{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{#- An EDTF value, but only when it is a plain ISO date: YYYY, YYYY-MM or
    YYYY-MM-DD. Anything qualified, any interval, set or unspecified digit
    returns ''. -#}
{% macro certain(raw) %}
{%- set s = (raw ?? '')|trim -%}
{%- if s matches '/^\\d{4}(-\\d{2}(-\\d{2})?)?$/' -%}{{ s }}{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{#- What to publish for a date that has both a stored EDTF value and a printed
    one.

    The EDTF field wins whenever it is filled, INCLUDING when what it says is
    "this is uncertain". That is the whole point, and it is the part that is easy
    to get wrong: falling back to parsing the printed form when the EDTF is
    qualified would re-publish the certainty through the back door. A record
    holding birthDateEdtf '1847?-03-27' and birthDate 'March 27, 1847' must emit
    nothing, not 1847-03-27.

    So: EDTF filled -> publish it only if certain, never fall back.
        EDTF empty   -> parse the printed form, as before. -#}
{% macro published(edtf, printed) %}
{#- The import must live INSIDE the macro: a macro body cannot see the
    template's own imports, so a top-level `{% import _self %}` compiles fine
    and then throws "Undefined array key" on every page that calls this. -#}
{%- import _self as d -%}
{%- set e = (edtf ?? '')|trim -%}
{%- if e is not empty -%}{{ d.certain(e) }}{%- else -%}{{ d.iso(printed ?? '') }}{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
